    {
      "id": "1d470f8463442fa6882aed23773a67995c4e45bf",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 652376566,
          "type": null,
          "body": "I don't think this is correct. I don't understand where this reasoning comes from (in commit 379352ef7f3e2be00ec2b16b84f0a2a424586af6): \"The PUBLIC keyword adds it to the INTERFACE_INCLUDE_DIRECTORIES property, which is only appropriate for includes that we ship with Wireshark\"\n\n\"Interface\" is specifically for transitive dependencies like GLib. If the hello plugin, or any other, doesn't use GLib, which should it need to link with it explicitly? That doesn't make sense to me.",
          "author": {
            "id": 2639362,
            "username": "jvalverde",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "João Valverde",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/bea5f745397cf5a2fe0526df96ec143db47f9334340ab56515038832be57e976?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/jvalverde"
          },
          "created_at": "2021-08-16T23:52:10.107Z",
          "updated_at": "2021-08-17T01:28:18.660Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 112564290,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": false,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 3891,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "300f5c16d1db4218b782b89cc68706c2508ddac4",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 652403589,
          "type": null,
          "body": "> \"Interface\" is specifically for transitive dependencies like GLib. If the hello plugin, or any other, doesn't use GLib, which should it need to link with it explicitly?\n\nIf program A depends on library B which depends on library C:\n\n- if they're all being statically linked, program A must be linked with library B, and that link command won't work unless library C is linked in as well (otherwise, routines that library B calls won't be found);\n- if they're all being dynamically linked, and if one dynamically-linked library is linked with another dynamically-linked library, if a program (or other dynamically-linked library) that's linked with the first library is used, the run-time linker will drag in the second library (i.e., if the run-time linker resolves transitive dependencies), program A need only be linked with library B;\n- if the run-time linker *doesn't* resolve transitive dependencies, program A would also need to be linked with library C as well.\n\nI think all UN\\*Xes we support, and Windows, have run-time linkers (or whatever the equivalent is on Windows) that resolve transitive dependencies.  If that's not the case, we're in the third situation in that list.",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2021-08-17T01:34:21.346Z",
          "updated_at": "2021-08-17T01:34:21.346Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 112564290,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": false,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 3891,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c7d1d4ec5c683e5b347c3da341eeec106af2bdb1",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 652404747,
          "type": null,
          "body": "In modern CMake, \"linking a library to a target\" also sets the include search path and other configuration. If program A depends on library B which depends on library C, and headers of B include headers of C, then we need target_link_library(A B) to add C's header directory to the include search path of A, otherwise it won't compile; regardless of the behavior of the linker or runtime-linker.",
          "author": {
            "id": 620868,
            "username": "nicolas17",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Nicolás Alvarez",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/82fc14a8d756131416a2f108bbdd6009b2ba9df642adc22729ba38ca05c8cc00?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/nicolas17"
          },
          "created_at": "2021-08-17T01:39:44.853Z",
          "updated_at": "2021-08-17T01:39:44.853Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 112564290,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": false,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 3891,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "dbe9655aa49728be0d866dfcf0e17e7f134e36c0",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 652426134,
          "type": null,
          "body": "> In modern CMake, \"linking a library to a target\" also sets the include search path and other configuration.\n\nThen it shouldn't be called \"linking a library to a target\" unless \"linking\" is interpreted as meaning something other than \"what the linker does\".\n\nIs CMake 3.5, which is the minimum we currently require on UN\\*Xes, sufficiently modern?",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2021-08-17T02:55:31.175Z",
          "updated_at": "2021-08-17T02:55:31.175Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 112564290,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": false,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 3891,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "4c97a2e15bdddfb10b107be94de13c6f67f18f23",
      "individual_note": false,
      "resolvable": true,
      "resolved": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 652884944,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "Adding to the above, in the context of CMake, linking means using the CMake command `target_link_libraries()`. This adds the include directory to the search path if needed. Tools like cmake-packages and pkg-config are intended to obviate the need for this change (configuring transitive dependencies explicitly), and it was working OK before commit 379352ef7f3e2be00ec2b16b84f0a2a424586af6.",
          "author": {
            "id": 2639362,
            "username": "jvalverde",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "João Valverde",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/bea5f745397cf5a2fe0526df96ec143db47f9334340ab56515038832be57e976?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/jvalverde"
          },
          "created_at": "2021-08-17T12:10:26.560Z",
          "updated_at": "2021-08-19T01:52:45.214Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 112564290,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": false,
          "resolved_by": null,
          "resolved_at": null,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 3891,
          "commands_changes": {}
        },
        {
          "id": 654585830,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "Do you know where it's supposed to add the include directory? The current master generates\n\n```\nset_target_properties(epan PROPERTIES\n  INTERFACE_INCLUDE_DIRECTORIES \"${_IMPORT_PREFIX}/include/wireshark\"\n  INTERFACE_LINK_LIBRARIES \"wiretap;wsutil\"\n)\n```\n\nin `CMakeFiles/Export/lib/wireshark/cmake/WiresharkTargets.cmake`. If I change \n\n```\ntarget_link_libraries(epan ... PRIVATE ... ${GLIB2_LIBRARIES}\n```\n\nto \n\n```\ntarget_link_libraries(epan ... PUBLIC ... ${GLIB2_LIBRARIES}\n```\n\nin `epan\\CMakeLists.txt` it generates\n\n```\nset_target_properties(epan PROPERTIES\n  INTERFACE_INCLUDE_DIRECTORIES \"${_IMPORT_PREFIX}/include/wireshark\"\n  INTERFACE_LINK_LIBRARIES \"wiretap;wsutil;/usr/local/Cellar/glib/2.68.3/lib/libglib-2.0.dylib\"\n)\n```\n\nI need to add `target_include_directories(epan SYSTEM PUBLIC ${GLIB2_INCLUDE_DIRS})` in order to add include paths:\n\n```\nset_target_properties(epan PROPERTIES\n  INTERFACE_INCLUDE_DIRECTORIES \"/usr/local/Cellar/glib/2.68.3/include/glib-2.0;/usr/local/Cellar/glib/2.68.3/lib/glib-2.0/include;${_IMPORT_PREFIX}/include/wireshark\"\n  INTERFACE_LINK_LIBRARIES \"wiretap;wsutil;/usr/local/Cellar/glib/2.68.3/lib/libglib-2.0.dylib\"\n  INTERFACE_SYSTEM_INCLUDE_DIRECTORIES \"/usr/local/Cellar/glib/2.68.3/include/glib-2.0;/usr/local/Cellar/glib/2.68.3/lib/glib-2.0/include\"\n)\n```\n\nI don't mind reverting all or part of 379352ef, but that would reintroduce the behavior in #17477. Maybe we care more about transitive dependencies than the issue described there?",
          "author": {
            "id": 860017,
            "username": "geraldcombs",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Gerald Combs",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://gitlab.com/uploads/-/system/user/avatar/860017/avatar.png?v=1789948913",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/geraldcombs"
          },
          "created_at": "2021-08-19T01:52:45.111Z",
          "updated_at": "2021-08-19T01:52:45.111Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 112564290,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": false,
          "resolved_by": null,
          "resolved_at": null,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 3891,
          "commands_changes": {}
        },
        {
          "id": 654606912,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "You're right, I misspoke when I mentioned `target_link_libraries()` without also mentioning `target_include_directories()`, I think both are needed, because GLib is not configured using the full \"modern CMake\" machinery, as you pointed out. Since GLIb doesn't support CMake upstream that would required changing FindGLIB2.cmake to create an \"imported\" library.\n\nAnyway, the relevant point is that both CMake commands are needed, not just `target_link_libraries()`. Sorry for the confusion there.\n \nBecause #17477 is specific to Windows I did not pay attention to it. Seems like a build dir below the source dir is incompatible with the Wireshark Windows libraries? I would have to look into that to see what can be done about it but I feel strongly that forcing plugin authors to handle transitive dependencies for epan is a way worse outcome in the end.",
          "author": {
            "id": 2639362,
            "username": "jvalverde",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "João Valverde",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/bea5f745397cf5a2fe0526df96ec143db47f9334340ab56515038832be57e976?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/jvalverde"
          },
          "created_at": "2021-08-19T02:50:45.907Z",
          "updated_at": "2021-08-19T02:50:45.907Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 112564290,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": false,
          "resolved_by": null,
          "resolved_at": null,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 3891,
          "commands_changes": {}
        },
        {
          "id": 655341791,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "> Because #17477 is specific to Windows I did not pay attention to it. Seems like a build dir below the source dir is incompatible with the Wireshark Windows libraries? I would have to look into that to see what can be done about it but I feel strongly that forcing plugin authors to handle transitive dependencies for epan is a way worse outcome in the end.\n\nI think the issue in #17477 is putting the windows **libraries** directory under the source directory, not the build directory. I don't have a Windows machine at the moment to test.\n\nMaybe it could be handled with a generator expression but \"avoid doing that, it's unsupported\" is also an adequate fix IMO.",
          "author": {
            "id": 2639362,
            "username": "jvalverde",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "João Valverde",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/bea5f745397cf5a2fe0526df96ec143db47f9334340ab56515038832be57e976?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/jvalverde"
          },
          "created_at": "2021-08-19T16:24:09.111Z",
          "updated_at": "2021-08-19T16:24:09.111Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 112564290,
